PR #499  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Pure-capability ABI enforces bounded va_list via x5 capability with Store/Execute cleared, preventing variadic argument over-read from leaking adjacent stack memory
  [RISK] include/linux/stdarg.h:5-9  (top-level macros)
      Central choke point with zero software-level bounds enforcement. All variadic builtins (va_start, va_arg, va_end, va_copy) delegate entirely to __builtin_va_* compiler intrinsics. There is no RISC-V arch override (no asm/va_list.h, no software va_list walker). On RVY pure-cap, correctness depends entirely on the compiler emitting a hardware capability with spatial bounds derived from the actual argument count at va_start. If the compiler instead sizes the capability to a fixed save-area or miscomputes the variadic argument count, all downstream va_arg consumers become unbounded. The kernel has no second layer of defense.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:164-167  handle_exception
      REG_S x5, PT_T0(sp) saves the t0 register to pt_regs on exception entry. REG_S resolves to 'sd' (64-bit store), which writes only the 64-bit integer address portion of the capability register. On RVY pure-cap, x5/t0 is a 129(ish)-bit capability (64-bit address + bounds base/length + permissions + validity tag). The 'sd' instruction silently truncates all capability metadata — the bounds, permissions, and tag are discarded. struct pt_regs stores 'unsigned long t0', confirming the raw-integer-only representation. This is not a theoretical issue: every exception entry destroys capability state in x5.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:304-308  ret_from_exception
      REG_L x5, PT_T0(sp) restores t0 from pt_regs via 'ld' (64-bit load). Since pt_regs stored only the raw address (the capability bounds were truncated on save), the restored x5 becomes an unbounded capability — the hardware will fabricate a default capability with full spatial authority (bounds covering the entire address space) around the loaded address. If x5 was previously holding a bounded va_list cursor or a bounded pointer, the restored register now carries unrestricted memory access authority. This is a privilege-escalation primitive: any code executing after exception return that uses t0 for memory access gets full spatial authority.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:335-338  handle_kernel_stack_overflow
      Same REG_S x5, PT_T0(sp) truncation as handle_exception, but on the kernel-stack-overflow code path (CONFIG_VMAP_STACK). This path saves t0 to the per-CPU overflow stack via 'sd', discarding capability bounds. The overflow path is rarely exercised, making this both harder to exploit and harder to notice in testing. However, if triggered (kernel stack exhaustion from deep recursion or large stack frames), the restored t0 loses all capability authority — same privilege-escalation primitive as the main exception path, just on a less-frequent code path.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c:441-443  setup_rt_frame
      BCFI shadow stack enforcement: 'regs->t0 = regs->ra' copies the return address into t0. Both regs->t0 and regs->ra are 'unsigned long' fields in struct pt_regs — raw integer values already stripped of capability metadata. The comment at line 441 ('if bcfi is enabled x1 (ra) and x5 (t0) must match') acknowledges that x1 and x5 carry a semantic relationship, but on RVY pure-cap this relationship exists at the capability level (both must carry identical bounds and validity tags). The assignment copies only the 64-bit address — if ra was a bounded capability (e.g., a sentry pointing to the sigreturn trampoline), t0 gets only the address without the sentry bounds. The hardware BCFI check in the return path (which compares full capability equality, not just address equality) would fail or be bypassed because t0 is a plain capability without bounds.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/probes/rethook_trampoline.S:13-18  arch_rethook_trampoline (save_all_base_regs macro)
      save_all_base_regs macro saves x5 (and all other GPRs) to pt_regs on the stack via REG_S (sd). On RVY pure-cap, x5/t0 capability bounds are truncated identically to the main exception path. This macro is invoked by arch_rethook_trampoline at line 80 when a kprobe/rethook fires — the probe context save/restore cycle silently strips capability metadata from x5. Any kprobe or rethook that fires while t0 holds a bounded capability (e.g., in the middle of a variadic function's va_arg sequence) corrupts that capability on return.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/probes/rethook_trampoline.S:49-49  arch_rethook_trampoline (restore_all_base_regs macro)
      restore_all_base_regs macro restores x5 from pt_regs via REG_L (ld). This is the restore counterpart to the capability-truncating save at line 17. The restored x5 becomes an unbounded capability — exactly the same privilege-escalation primitive as ret_from_exception. The save/restore asymmetry (save truncates -> restore fabricates unbounded) means any kprobe instrumenting code that uses t0 for bounded access silently widens the capability authority on return.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:71-71  __kvm_riscv_switch_to (via RESTORE_GUEST_GPRS macro)
      Restores guest t0 from KVM context struct via REG_L (ld). The KVM save area stores raw register values (the architecture uses 'unsigned long' fields). Guest capability bounds — set by the guest VM's code — are lost. The restored t0 becomes an unbounded capability in the guest context. This is a guest-visible corruption: if the guest uses t0 for bounded va_list traversal, after a VM exit+reentry cycle, t0's bounds are replaced with infinite bounds. More critically, if the guest had intentionally narrowed t0's bounds (e.g., sandboxing), the VM exit/entry cycle defeats that sandbox.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:111-111  __kvm_riscv_switch_to (via SAVE_GUEST_GPRS macro)
      Saves guest t0 to KVM context struct via REG_S (sd). All guest capability bounds, permissions, and validity tags are silently discarded at the VM exit boundary. The host never sees the guest's true capability state — only raw addresses. From a host defense perspective, this is a missing introspection point: the host cannot verify whether the guest's t0 capability was legitimately bounded or was corrupted/forged. The save area must be extended to store full capability width (address + metadata + tag) for the host to correctly virtualize capability registers.
