PR #499  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Pure-capability ABI enforces bounded va_list via x5 capability with Store/Execute cleared, preventing variadic argument over-read from leaking adjacent stack memory
  [RISK] sys/sys/_stdarg.h:36-47  (global scope)
      va_list is unconditionally typedef'd to __builtin_va_list with no __CHERI__ guard. On purecap targets (riscv64c, aarch64c), the correctness of spatial bounds on the variadic argument region depends entirely on whether the compiler's __builtin_va_list emits a bounded capability. No compile-time assertion checks sizeof(va_list) == 16 or that the builtin is capability-aware. By contrast, __intptr_t/__uintptr_t at sys/sys/_types.h:70-76 explicitly test __CHERI__ and promote to __intcap_t/__uintcap_t. The inconsistency means the kernel has no defence-in-depth if a non-CHERI compiler or misconfigured toolchain is used for a purecap build.
  [RISK] sys/sys/_types.h:251-254  (global scope)
      __va_list (line 251) and __gnuc_va_list (line 254) are unconditionally defined from __builtin_va_list without any __CHERI__ conditional. This is the kernel-internal va_list type used throughout the kernel when variadic functions are written. The same header file, just 180 lines earlier (lines 70-76), explicitly promotes intptr_t/uintptr_t to __intcap_t/__uintcap_t under __CHERI__ — establishing a clear project convention that pointer-width types must be capability-aware. The omission here is structurally conspicuous: every pointer-like type gets a CHERI guard except va_list.
